# Generated by fwm on @DATE@:
#   @COMMAND@
# fwm-run: @RUN_INFO@
# run.json describes what the run uses: its sysroot, entry point, trace and checkpoint.
#
# Override any variable on the make command line, e.g.
#   make run SIM=/path/to/spike SIM_FLAGS_EXTRA=-e100000000
#
# spike options (-e, -s ...) go in SIM_FLAGS_EXTRA, FESVR options (+verbose ...) in FESVR_FLAGS_EXTRA. -m and -p are
# set from the entry point: a replay or a checkpoint needs the same ones.
#
# spike's exit status:
#   - without -e, it is the program's exit status (0 after creating the last checkpoint);
#   - with -e<n>, it is 1 when the run was stopped after n instructions (make then reports "Error 1"), and 0 when the
#     program ended first, whatever its own exit status;
#   - an error of FESVR (e.g. a replay that diverged from its recording) aborts spike.
# final-state.json says why the run finished (finish_reason) and, unless the FESVR failed, each hart's instruction count
# there: target_exit (with exit_code), checkpoints_created, instret_limit (-e), interrupted (SIGTERM/SIGINT, with
# signal: spike then exits 0), error (with the FESVR's message), or unfinished (spike died). Judge a run by it, not by
# make's exit status.

SIM ?= @SIM@
SIM_FLAGS_EXTRA ?=
FESVR_FLAGS_EXTRA ?=

# The run directory: every path is absolute
RUN := @RUN@
